\title{Genuinely Dependent Fibre Families of\\ One-Variable Tropical Polynomials}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{13 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $p(x)=\min_{0\le i\le n}(c_i+ix)$ be the min-plus (tropical) polynomial of degree
$n$ with rational coefficient vector $c=(c_0,\dots,c_n)$. Its \emph{fibre} at a point
$x$ is the set $F_{n,c}(x)=\{i\le n: c_i+ix=p(x)\}$ of exponents attaining the minimum;
its cardinality is the local multiplicity of $p$ at $x$. We study the parametrised
family of finite sets $x\mapsto F_{n,c}(x)$ and ask whether it can be \emph{pointwise
equivalent} to a constant family, i.e.\ whether a fixed finite set $T$ can admit a
bijection $F_{n,c}(x)\simeq T$ for every $x$. We prove that the answer is no in the
strongest form: for \emph{every} $n\ge 1$ and \emph{every} coefficient vector the fibre
family is not pointwise equivalent to a constant family, with the explicit witnesses
$x^{\star}=\max_{1\le i\le n}(c_0-c_i)/i$ and $x^{\star}+1$, and with no genericity or
convexity hypothesis. We then determine exactly which patterns occur: for every $k$ with
$2\le k\le n+1$ an explicit step polynomial has a fibre of cardinality $k$ alongside a
fibre of cardinality $1$, and $k\le n+1$ is optimal. A single monotone ordering law
--- winning exponents decrease as the argument increases --- yields almost-disjointness
of fibres, a window bound, the degree bound $\sum_{x\in S}(\card{F(x)}-1)\le n$ over
arbitrary finite $S$, finiteness of the corner set, and the bound of $n$ on the number of
corners. For convex coefficient data, given by a nondecreasing sequence of
Newton-polygon increments, we compute fibres exactly: the fibre at $-v$ is the closed
index window carrying slope $v$, so its cardinality is one more than the number of
increments equal to $v$; summing over slopes turns the degree bound into an equality.
Consequently the multiplicity profile of a convex degree-$n$ tropical polynomial is
precisely a composition of $n$, and every composition of $n$ is realised by an explicit
staircase polynomial. Finally, the equality genuinely needs convexity: for
$c=(0,5,1,7)$ of degree $3$ the total multiplicity excess is $2<3$, the defect counting
the lattice points of the lower hull unoccupied by monomials. We contrast the
polynomial fibre family with a min-plus divisor-lattice aggregate whose argmin family
\emph{is} constant, showing that non-constancy is a feature of crossing lines rather
than of tropicalisation as such.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} tropical polynomial, min-plus algebra, fibre family, local
multiplicity, Newton polygon, composition, tropical fundamental theorem,
piecewise-linear optimisation.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Tropical polynomials as lower envelopes}

The min-plus semiring $(\QQ\cup\{+\infty\},\oplus,\odot)$ replaces addition by minimum,
$a\oplus b=\min(a,b)$, and multiplication by addition, $a\odot b=a+b$. Its additive
identity is $+\infty$ and its multiplicative identity is $0$. A polynomial in this
semiring,
\[
  p=\bigoplus_{i=0}^{n} c_i\odot x^{\odot i},
\]
becomes, translated back into ordinary arithmetic, the function
\[
  p(x)\;=\;\min_{0\le i\le n}\bigl(c_i+ix\bigr).
\]
A tropical polynomial of degree $n$ is therefore the pointwise minimum of $n+1$ affine
functions whose slopes are the exponents $0,1,\dots,n$: a piecewise-linear concave
function determined by finitely many breakpoints. This concreteness is what makes
tropical geometry effective; the invariant relevant here is \emph{multiplicity}, which
in the tropical world is the lattice length of an edge of the Newton polygon.

\subsection{The fibre family}

Throughout, $n\in\NN$ and $c:\NN\to\QQ$ is an arbitrary coefficient function (only its
values on $\{0,\dots,n\}$ matter).

\begin{definition}[Tropical evaluation]
$\tv_{n,c}(x)=\min_{0\le i\le n}(c_i+ix)$, the minimum over the nonempty index range
$\{0,\dots,n\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fibre]
The \emph{fibre} of $p$ at $x\in\QQ$ is
\[
  F_{n,c}(x)\;=\;\bigl\{\,i\in\{0,1,\dots,n\}\;:\;c_i+ix=\tv_{n,c}(x)\,\bigr\}.
\]
Its cardinality $\card{F_{n,c}(x)}$ is the \emph{local multiplicity} of $p$ at $x$.
\end{definition}

The map $x\mapsto F_{n,c}(x)$ is a family of finite sets indexed by the tropical line.
Away from finitely many points it is a singleton recording which monomial is active; at
breakpoints it records how many monomials collide.

\begin{definition}[Pointwise equivalence]
Two families $F,G$ of finite subsets of $\NN$ indexed by $\QQ$ are \emph{pointwise
equivalent} if for every $x$ there is a bijection $F(x)\to G(x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Constant family]
A family $G$ is \emph{constant} if there is a finite set $T$ with $G(x)=T$ for all $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Genuinely dependent]
A family $F$ is \emph{genuinely dependent} if no constant family is pointwise equivalent
to it.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Cardinality criterion]\label{lem:crit}
$F$ is genuinely dependent if and only if there exist $x,y$ with
$\card{F(x)}\ne\card{F(y)}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A bijection $F(x)\to G(x)=T$ gives $\card{F(x)}=\card{T}$ for every $x$, so pointwise
equivalence to a constant family implies constant cardinality. Conversely, if
$\card{F(x)}=k$ for all $x$ then $F$ is pointwise equivalent to the constant family with
value $\{1,\dots,k\}$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Relation to the tropical fundamental theorem}

Classically, a tropical polynomial of degree $n$ has exactly $n$ roots counted with
multiplicity, the roots being the breakpoints and the multiplicity of a breakpoint the
lattice length of the corresponding Newton-polygon edge. Theorem~\ref{thm:degree} below
is the inequality half, valid with no hypotheses on $c$; Theorem~\ref{thm:equality} is
the equality half, valid exactly in the convex regime; and Section~\ref{sec:strict}
shows that some hypothesis is genuinely necessary. Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum} classifies
the possible ordered multiplicity profiles as exactly the compositions of $n$.

\section{Basic calculus of fibres}

\begin{lemma}[Membership]\label{lem:mem}
$i\in F_{n,c}(x)$ if and only if $i\le n$ and $c_i+ix\le c_j+jx$ for every $j\le n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The minimum is $\le c_i+ix$ for $i\le n$, and any lower bound for all monomial values is
$\le$ the minimum; so $c_i+ix=\tv_{n,c}(x)$ is equivalent to $c_i+ix$ being such a lower
bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Non-emptiness and cap]\label{lem:cap}
$F_{n,c}(x)\ne\varnothing$, and $1\le\card{F_{n,c}(x)}\le n+1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The minimum of a nonempty finite set of rationals is attained, and
$F_{n,c}(x)\subseteq\{0,\dots,n\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone ordering law]\label{thm:order}
Let $x<y$. If $i\in F_{n,c}(x)$ and $j\in F_{n,c}(y)$, then $j\le i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:mem}, $c_i+ix\le c_j+jx$ and $c_j+jy\le c_i+iy$. Adding and cancelling
the coefficients gives $ix+jy\le jx+iy$, i.e.\ $(j-i)(y-x)\le 0$. Since $y-x>0$ we
obtain $j\le i$.
\end{proof}

Geometrically this is concavity: the active slope of the lower envelope is
nonincreasing in $x$.

\begin{corollary}[Almost disjointness]\label{cor:disj}
If $x\ne y$ then $\card{F_{n,c}(x)\cap F_{n,c}(y)}\le 1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Say $x<y$ and let $a,b$ lie in both fibres. Theorem~\ref{thm:order} with $a\in F(y)$,
$b\in F(x)$ gives $a\le b$; with roles reversed, $b\le a$. Hence $a=b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Index window]
Write $\lo(x)=\min F_{n,c}(x)$ and $\hi(x)=\max F_{n,c}(x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Window bound]\label{lem:window}
$\card{F_{n,c}(x)}\le\hi(x)-\lo(x)+1$, and $\hi(x)\le n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$F_{n,c}(x)$ is contained in the integer interval $[\lo(x),\hi(x)]$, which has
$\hi(x)-\lo(x)+1$ elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Windows are stacked]\label{lem:stack}
If $x<y$ then $\hi(y)\le\lo(x)$; and if $x\le y$ then $\lo(y)\le\hi(x)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The first is Theorem~\ref{thm:order} applied to $\lo(x)\in F(x)$ and $\hi(y)\in F(y)$.
For the second, when $x<y$ chain $\lo(y)\le\hi(y)\le\lo(x)\le\hi(x)$; when $x=y$ it is
trivial.
\end{proof}

Thus the windows of distinct points are back-to-back intervals in $\{0,\dots,n\}$,
ordered oppositely to the points and overlapping in at most an endpoint.

\section{Corners always exist}

\begin{theorem}[Corner existence]\label{thm:corner}
Let $n\ge 1$ and let $c$ be arbitrary. Put
\[
  x^{\star}\;=\;\max_{1\le i\le n}\frac{c_0-c_i}{i}.
\]
Then $\card{F_{n,c}(x^{\star})}\ge 2$ and $F_{n,c}(x^{\star}+1)=\{0\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $i_0\in\{1,\dots,n\}$ attain the maximum, so $x^{\star}=(c_0-c_{i_0})/i_0$.

\emph{Index $0$ wins at $x^{\star}$.} For $1\le i\le n$, $(c_0-c_i)/i\le x^{\star}$ and
$i>0$ give $c_0\le c_i+ix^{\star}$; for $i=0$ this is trivial. By Lemma~\ref{lem:mem},
$0\in F_{n,c}(x^{\star})$.

\emph{Index $i_0$ also wins.} Clearing denominators in $x^{\star}=(c_0-c_{i_0})/i_0$
gives $c_{i_0}+i_0x^{\star}=c_0$, so $i_0$ attains the same value as the (winning)
index $0$; hence $i_0\in F_{n,c}(x^{\star})$ and the fibre has at least two elements.

\emph{Index $0$ wins alone at $x^{\star}+1$.} For $1\le j\le n$,
\[
  c_0<c_0+j\le c_j+jx^{\star}+j=c_j+j(x^{\star}+1),
\]
strictly because $j\ge 1$. So $0$ is the unique minimiser.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Main theorem: genuine dependence]\label{thm:main}
For every $n\ge 1$ and every coefficient vector $c$, the family $x\mapsto F_{n,c}(x)$ is
genuinely dependent: there is no constant family pointwise equivalent to it.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $G(x)=T$ for all $x$ and $F_{n,c}(x)\simeq G(x)$ for all $x$; then
$\card{F_{n,c}(x)}=\card{T}$ for all $x$. With $x^{\star}$ as in
Theorem~\ref{thm:corner}, $\card{T}=\card{F_{n,c}(x^{\star})}\ge 2$ and
$\card{T}=\card{F_{n,c}(x^{\star}+1)}=1$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Three features deserve emphasis. First, there are \emph{no} hypotheses on $c$: the
statement is not that witnesses \emph{can be chosen} with two fibres of unequal
cardinality, but that every polynomial already exhibits this. Second, the proof is
constructive: the witnesses are computed from the coefficients by one maximum over $n$
rationals. Third, the right-hand witness has fibre exactly $\{0\}$.

\section{Corners are rare: the degree bound}

\begin{definition}[Corner set]
$\Corn(n,c)=\{x\in\QQ:\card{F_{n,c}(x)}\ge 2\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Telescoping bound]\label{prop:tele}
For every nonempty finite $S\subset\QQ$, writing $a=\min S$ and $b=\max S$,
\[
  \sum_{x\in S}\bigl(\card{F_{n,c}(x)}-1\bigr)\;\le\;\hi(a)-\lo(b).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Induct on $\card{S}$. For $S=\{a\}$ the claim is Lemma~\ref{lem:window}. Otherwise let
$S'=S\setminus\{a\}$ with $a'=\min S'$ and $\max S'=b$. Then
\[
  \sum_{x\in S}(\card{F(x)}-1)\le\bigl(\hi(a)-\lo(a)\bigr)+\bigl(\hi(a')-\lo(b)\bigr)
\]
by Lemma~\ref{lem:window} on $a$ and induction on $S'$. Since $a<a'$,
Lemma~\ref{lem:stack} gives $\hi(a')\le\lo(a)$, and the right-hand side collapses to
$\hi(a)-\lo(b)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Degree bound]\label{thm:degree}
For every $n$, every $c$, and every finite $S\subset\QQ$,
\[
  \sum_{x\in S}\bigl(\card{F_{n,c}(x)}-1\bigr)\;\le\;n.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $S=\varnothing$ the sum is $0$; otherwise apply Proposition~\ref{prop:tele} together
with $\hi(\min S)\le n$ and $\lo(\max S)\ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finitely many corners]\label{thm:count}
$\Corn(n,c)$ is finite and $\card{\Corn(n,c)}\le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each corner contributes at least $1$ to the sum of Theorem~\ref{thm:degree}. If there
were $n+1$ distinct corners, taking $S$ to be that set would give
$n+1\le\sum_{x\in S}(\card{F(x)}-1)\le n$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $n\ge 1$ the fibre family equals $1$ outside at most $n$ points, exceeds $1$
somewhere, and never exceeds $n+1$: it is genuinely dependent but only finitely so.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
For the totally degenerate polynomial $c\equiv 0$, $F_{n,c}(0)=\{0,\dots,n\}$, so the
bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:degree} is attained with a single corner of multiplicity
$n+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At $x=0$ every monomial value is $c_i=0$, so all $n+1$ indices tie.
\end{proof}

\section{Every admissible cardinality is realised}

\begin{definition}[Step polynomial]
For $k\ge 1$ let $\sigma_k(i)=0$ if $i<k$ and $\sigma_k(i)=1$ if $i\ge k$. The degree-$n$
step polynomial of width $k$ is $\min_{i\le n}(\sigma_k(i)+ix)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:step0}
If $1\le k\le n+1$ then $F_{n,\sigma_k}(0)=\{0,1,\dots,k-1\}$, of cardinality $k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At $x=0$ the monomial value at $i$ is $\sigma_k(i)\in\{0,1\}$; since $\sigma_k(0)=0$ the
minimum is $0$, attained exactly when $i<k$, and all such $i$ are admissible because
$k\le n+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:step1}
If $k\ge 1$ then $F_{n,\sigma_k}(1)=\{0\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At $x=1$ the value at $i$ is $\sigma_k(i)+i$, which is $0$ at $i=0$ and at least $1$ for
$i\ge 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dependent solutions at every nontrivial finite cardinality]
\label{thm:everyk}
For all $n$ and all $k$ with $2\le k\le n+1$ there is a degree-$n$ tropical polynomial
with a fibre of cardinality exactly $k$ and a fibre of cardinality exactly $1$; the
range $k\le n+1$ is optimal by Lemma~\ref{lem:cap}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $c=\sigma_k$, $x=0$, $y=1$ and apply Lemmas~\ref{lem:step0} and~\ref{lem:step1}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $n\ge 2$ both multiplicities $2$ and $3$ occur, each alongside a singleton fibre;
and $\card{F_{n,\sigma_{n+1}}(0)}=n+1$ realises the maximum.
\end{corollary}

\section{Convex data: exact fibres from the Newton polygon}

\begin{definition}[Increment presentation]
For $d:\NN\to\QQ$ set $C_d(i)=\sum_{\ell<i}d_\ell$, so $C_d(i+1)-C_d(i)=d_i$. Call $d$
\emph{convex} if it is nondecreasing; then the points $(i,C_d(i))$ all lie on the lower
convex hull, with edge slopes $d_0\le d_1\le\cdots$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Telescoping identity]\label{lem:teleid}
For $i\le j$,
\[
  \bigl(C_d(j)+jx\bigr)-\bigl(C_d(i)+ix\bigr)=\sum_{i\le\ell<j}\bigl(d_\ell+x\bigr).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The coefficient difference is $\sum_{i\le\ell<j}d_\ell$ and the linear difference is
$(j-i)x=\sum_{i\le\ell<j}x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact fibre, window form]\label{thm:windowfibre}
Let $A\le B\le n$ and $v\in\QQ$ satisfy $d_\ell<v$ for $\ell<A$, $d_\ell=v$ for
$A\le\ell<B$, and $d_\ell>v$ for $B\le\ell<n$. Then
$F_{n,C_d}(-v)=\{A,A+1,\dots,B\}$, so $\card{F_{n,C_d}(-v)}=B-A+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $V(i)=C_d(i)-iv$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:teleid}, $V(j)-V(i)=\sum_{i\le\ell<j}(d_\ell-v)$
for $i\le j$. For $i<A$ all terms of $V(A)-V(i)$ are strictly negative, so $V(A)<V(i)$.
For $A\le i\le B$ all terms vanish, so $V(i)=V(A)$. For $B<i\le n$ all terms of
$V(i)-V(B)$ are strictly positive, so $V(i)>V(A)$. Hence the minimum is $V(A)$ and is
attained exactly on $\{A,\dots,B\}$.
\end{proof}

For convex $d$ the hypotheses hold automatically with
\[
  A=\#\{\ell<n:d_\ell<v\},\qquad B=\#\{\ell<n:d_\ell\le v\},
\]
since monotonicity makes both sets initial segments of $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$.

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicity equals slope count plus one]\label{thm:mult}
Let $d$ be convex. For every $v\in\QQ$,
\[
  \card{F_{n,C_d}(-v)}=\#\{\ell<n:d_\ell=v\}+1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:windowfibre} with $A,B$ as above; the three hypotheses follow from
the initial-segment description, and $B-A=\#\{\ell<n:d_\ell=v\}$.
\end{proof}

In Newton-polygon language: the multiplicity of a corner is one more than the lattice
length of the corresponding hull edge. In particular $\card{F(-v)}=1$ whenever $v$ is
not a slope, so corners occur exactly at the negatives of the slopes.

\begin{theorem}[Fundamental theorem, equality form]\label{thm:equality}
Let $d$ be convex and $\mathcal{V}=\{d_\ell:\ell<n\}$ the set of distinct slopes. Then
\[
  \sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}}\bigl(\card{F_{n,C_d}(-v)}-1\bigr)=n .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:mult} the $v$-th summand is $\#\{\ell<n:d_\ell=v\}$, and these sets
partition $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The generic polynomial}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{ramp} coefficients are $\rho_i=\binom{i}{2}=i(i-1)/2$, with increments
$d_\ell=\ell$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Exact difference formula]\label{lem:ramp}
For all $m,j\in\NN$,
\[
  \bigl(\rho_j-jm\bigr)-\bigl(\rho_m-m^2\bigr)=\frac{(j-m)(j-m-1)}{2}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Expand and substitute $t=j-m$; the left-hand side simplifies to $t(t-1)/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Generic corners]\label{thm:generic}
For $m+1\le n$, $F_{n,\rho}(-m)=\{m,m+1\}$. Hence the degree-$n$ ramp polynomial has
exactly $n$ corners, at $0,-1,\dots,-(n-1)$, each simple, and
$\sum_{m=0}^{n-1}(\card{F_{n,\rho}(-m)}-1)=n$: both Theorem~\ref{thm:degree} and
Theorem~\ref{thm:count} are attained simultaneously.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $t(t-1)\ge 0$ for every integer $t$, with equality exactly at $t\in\{0,1\}$,
Lemma~\ref{lem:ramp} shows the minimum at $x=-m$ is attained exactly at $j=m$ and
$j=m+1$, both admissible when $m+1\le n$. The corner count is bounded above by
Theorem~\ref{thm:count}.
\end{proof}

\section{The composition spectrum}

A \emph{composition} of $n$ is an ordered tuple $(m_0,\dots,m_{r-1})$ of positive
integers with $\sum_j m_j=n$.

\begin{definition}[Staircase of a composition]
Given block sizes $m_0,\dots,m_{r-1}$, set $M_j=m_0+\cdots+m_{j-1}$ (so $M_0=0$,
$M_r=n$) and define the \emph{staircase increment function}
\[
  d^{(m)}_\ell=\#\{\,j<r:M_{j+1}\le\ell\,\},
\]
the index of the block containing position $\ell$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Convexity and block counting]\label{lem:stair}
$d^{(m)}$ is nondecreasing, and $\#\{\ell<n:d^{(m)}_\ell=j\}=m_j$ for every $j<r$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Increasing $\ell$ can only enlarge the counted set, whence monotonicity. Since $M$ is
nondecreasing, $d^{(m)}_\ell=j$ holds exactly when $M_j\le\ell<M_{j+1}$, an interval of
$m_j$ integers, all below $M_r=n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Every composition is realised]\label{thm:realise}
Let $(m_0,\dots,m_{r-1})$ be a composition of $n$ and $P=C_{d^{(m)}}$. Then
$\card{F_{n,P}(-j)}=m_j+1$ for every $j<r$, and
$\sum_{j<r}(\card{F_{n,P}(-j)}-1)=n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:stair} the increments are convex with exactly $m_j$ positions of slope
$j$; Theorem~\ref{thm:mult} at $v=j$ gives $\card{F(-j)}=m_j+1$, and summing gives $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Composition spectrum]\label{thm:spectrum}
A tuple of positive integers is the ordered multiplicity-excess profile of a convex
degree-$n$ tropical polynomial if and only if it is a composition of $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Necessity is Theorem~\ref{thm:equality}; sufficiency is Theorem~\ref{thm:realise}.
\end{proof}

Specialisations recover earlier results: all blocks of size $1$ gives the generic
staircase with $n$ simple corners; a single block of size $n$ gives the degenerate
corner of Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}; and two blocks give, for each $0\le m\le n$, a
degree-$n$ polynomial with exactly two corners of multiplicities $m+1$ and $n-m+1$
(concretely, increments $1$ on the first $m$ positions and $2$ thereafter put those
corners at $x=-1$ and $x=-2$).

\begin{corollary}[Prescribed unequal pair]
For every $n\ge 3$ and every $1\le m\le n-1$ with $m\ne n-m$ there is a degree-$n$
tropical polynomial two of whose fibres have the prescribed unequal cardinalities $m+1$
and $n-m+1$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Failure of equality without convexity}\label{sec:strict}

\begin{example}\label{ex:strict}
Let $c=(0,5,1,7)$, i.e.\ $p(x)=\min(0,\;5+x,\;1+2x,\;7+3x)$.
At $x=-\tfrac12$ the monomial values are $0,\,4.5,\,0,\,5.5$, so
$F_3(-\tfrac12)=\{0,2\}$. At $x=-6$ they are $0,\,-1,\,-11,\,-11$, so
$F_3(-6)=\{2,3\}$. These are the only corners: the envelope consists of a slope-$0$ ray
(index $0$), a slope-$2$ segment (index $2$) and a slope-$3$ ray (index $3$); index $1$
is never active. Hence
\[
  \sum_{x}\bigl(\card{F_3(x)}-1\bigr)=1+1=2<3=n .
\]
\end{example}

\begin{proposition}[Interpretation of the defect]
In Example~\ref{ex:strict} the point $(1,5)$ lies strictly above the segment from
$(0,0)$ to $(2,1)$, which has height $\tfrac12$ at abscissa $1$; the monomial $i=1$ is
therefore never the strict minimum. The hull edge from $(0,0)$ to $(2,1)$ has lattice
length $2$ but supports only excess $1$, because its interior lattice point is not
occupied by a monomial. The defect $n-\sum_x(\card{F(x)}-1)=1$ counts exactly the
missing lattice points.
\end{proposition}

Thus the degree bound is an equality exactly when the coefficient vector is
\emph{lattice-convex}, i.e.\ every lattice point of the lower hull is occupied by a
monomial attaining the hull; the convex presentation $c_i=\sum_{\ell<i}d_\ell$ with
nondecreasing $d$ guarantees this. The inequality remains universally valid and the
shortfall is itself a meaningful invariant.

\section{A contrasting constant family}

\begin{definition}
Let $w:\NN\to\NN$ be a weight. For $N\ge 1$ let
$A_w(N)=\{d\mid N: w(d)\le w(e)\text{ for all }e\mid N\}$ be the set of divisors
attaining the min-plus aggregate $\bigoplus_{d\mid N}w(d)=\min_{d\mid N}w(d)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
If $w$ is strictly increasing then $A_w(N)=\{1\}$ for every $N\ge 1$; in particular this
family \emph{is} pointwise equivalent to a constant family.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$1\mid N$ and $w(1)\le w(e)$ for all $e\ge 1$, so $1\in A_w(N)$. Conversely
$d\in A_w(N)$ forces $w(d)\le w(1)$, hence $d\le 1$ by strict monotonicity, hence $d=1$.
\end{proof}

The divisor lattice has a bottom element dividing every $N$ and minimising a monotone
weight uniformly, so one candidate dominates globally. In the polynomial setting the
candidates are affine functions of distinct slopes and any two of them cross:
non-constancy is a consequence of the geometry of crossing lines, not of min-plus
algebra as such.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Fibre evaluation.} Given $n$, $c$ and $x$, evaluate the $n+1$ rationals
$v_i=c_i+ix$ in exact arithmetic, take the minimum and return all indices attaining it.
Cost $\Theta(n)$. Exact arithmetic is essential: ties are precisely the interesting
events and are detected unreliably in floating point.

\paragraph{Corner enumeration by lower-hull scan.} The active indices are the vertices
of the lower convex hull of $\{(i,c_i)\}_{i=0}^{n}$, and the corner between consecutive
hull vertices $(i,c_i)$ and $(j,c_j)$ lies at $x=-(c_j-c_i)/(j-i)$, the negative of the
hull-edge slope. The fibre there is the set of occupied hull points on the edge, which
for a lattice-convex vector is the full window $\{i,\dots,j\}$. Since the abscissae are
already sorted, a monotone-chain scan gives all corners in $\Theta(n)$ time, together
with the total multiplicity excess (whose comparison with $n$ detects the
lattice-convexity defect) and the realised composition. Correctness follows from
Theorem~\ref{thm:windowfibre} edge by edge.

\paragraph{Composition realisation.} Given a composition, compute prefix sums $M_j$,
build the staircase $d^{(m)}$ in one pass, and take prefix sums again to obtain $c$.
Cost $\Theta(n)$; correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:realise}.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\paragraph{Parametric optimisation.} Read $i$ as an option with fixed cost $c_i$ and
sensitivity $i$ to a parameter $x$, and $p(x)$ as the optimal cost.
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} says a tie is unavoidable somewhere; Theorem~\ref{thm:count} says
ambiguous parameter values number at most $n$; Theorem~\ref{thm:order} is the classical
monotone-policy structure of parametric linear programming.

\paragraph{Piecewise-linear models.} A one-dimensional rectified-linear response is
exactly a min/max of affine functions. The fibre family is the activation pattern, the
degree bound caps the breakpoint count, and Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum} says any
prescribed pattern of breakpoint multiplicities is realisable by a choice of biases. The
defect of Example~\ref{ex:strict} is the shadow of dead units, whose affine pieces never
attain the envelope.

\paragraph{Lagrangian curves.} Sweeping a multiplier across a finite family of affine
lower bounds --- as in the Lagrangian dual of a finite rate--distortion problem, where
each admissible configuration contributes $s\mapsto I+sD$ --- produces exactly the
min-plus envelope studied here. The corners are the multiplier values at which the
optimiser switches, and the ordering law makes the optimal slope monotone in the
multiplier.

\paragraph{Tropical curves.} The corners are the roots and the multiplicities are the
lattice lengths of the Newton-polygon edges; Theorem~\ref{thm:equality} is the tropical
fundamental theorem of algebra in this dictionary, Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum} classifies
the possible root multiplicity data, and Section~\ref{sec:strict} quantifies how a
non-lattice-convex coefficient vector loses roots.

\section{Discussion}

Three themes organise the results. First, \emph{one mechanism}: the ordering law is the
sole structural input, and from it flow almost-disjointness, the window bound, stacking,
telescoping, the degree and corner-count bounds, and --- combined with the telescoping
identity --- the interval description of convex fibres. Second, \emph{universality then
quantification}: the original question asked whether witnesses can be chosen with two
fibres of unequal cardinality, and the answer is stronger, since no choice is needed;
only then does the quantitative question become interesting, and there the answer is
complete. Third, \emph{a false statement corrected}: the naive claim that the total
multiplicity excess always equals the degree is false, the correct universal statement
being the inequality with equality characterised by convexity, and the gap counting the
monomials that never attain the envelope.

The theory is one-variable. In several variables the corner set is a polyhedral complex
dual to a regular subdivision of the Newton polytope, the ordering law has no direct
analogue because argmin is not compatible with any total order on exponent vectors, and
extending the classification is a genuinely different problem. Working over $\QQ$ rather
than $\mathbb{R}$ is immaterial except that corner locations are then rational.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Lattice-length defect.} For a non-convex coefficient vector the corner
multiplicity can be strictly smaller than the lattice length of the corresponding
Newton-polygon edge, and the total defect is $n-\sum_x(\card{F(x)}-1)$. The key insight
is that the defect counts exactly the lattice points of the lower hull that are not
monomials of the polynomial, i.e.\ the failure of the coefficient vector to be
lattice-convex. Both sides of the proposed identity are already available --- the
hull-free inequality and the convex equality --- so the defect formula would upgrade the
inequality to an exact accounting valid for every coefficient vector.

\paragraph{Stability under perturbation.} The multiplicity profile is upper
semicontinuous in the coefficients: a small generic perturbation splits a corner of
multiplicity $m+1$ into $m$ simple corners. Quantifying the perturbation needed to fully
resolve a corner, in terms of the coefficient gaps, would give a tropical analogue of
root-separation bounds.

\paragraph{Multivariable fibre families.} Replace the tropical line by $\QQ^k$; the
corner locus becomes a polyhedral complex dual to a regular subdivision of the Newton
polytope and the degree bound should be replaced by a count of maximal cells. A new
mechanism is needed in place of the ordering law; the natural candidate is the
regular-subdivision duality itself.

\paragraph{Algorithmic profile inversion.} Given a target composition together with
constraints (integer coefficients, bounded size, prescribed corner locations), decide
realisability and produce a witness. The staircase construction solves the unconstrained
problem in linear time; under integrality and location constraints the problem becomes a
lattice-point feasibility question whose complexity is open.

